From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jens Axboe <axboe@kernel.dk>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 11:16:19 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] i2c: pasemi: report a NACK as a NACK while the STOP is
 still going out

With the interrupt enabled for MTN, the transfer wait returns as soon as
the device has not acknowledged a byte. The controller has not put the
STOP on the bus yet at that point, so SMSTA usually still has XIP set
(0x10210106: XIP, MTN, MTE), and since XIP is tested first the transfer
is reported as "Bus stuck" and fails with -EIO. Read a little later the
same event gives 0x08210106 and "NACK"; both were seen for the same
unpowered device within one boot of an M5 Pro MacBook Pro.

Nothing is stuck there: the controller finishes the STOP by itself and
the next transfer waits for the bus to go idle, resetting the controller
if it does not. Test MTN (and arbitration loss) before XIP so that an
unanswered address is logged as what it is and returns -ENXIO, which is
what clients probing for a device expect. A transfer that ends with XIP
set and no NACK is still reported as stuck.

Signed-off-by: Jens Axboe <axboe@kernel.dk>
Signed-off-by: Thomas Glanzmann <thomas@glanzmann.de>
---
omarchy-m5p-oled note (not part of the upstream commit):
Source: aurora-silicon/linux commit 48243e8f3d32b0cdcf2deef44172bf1a9df77ce0, pull request 1787
(open on 2026-11-14), unchanged. Tested upstream on a K116s ("11 of 11 boots clean").
Why here: the I2C controller of every bus on this Mac, the jack codec's included. A device that
does not answer is reported as a NACK (-ENXIO) and no longer as "Bus stuck" (-EIO).

 drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-pasemi-core.c | 19 ++++++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-pasemi-core.c b/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-pasemi-core.c
index 7105fc460..38bddbbce 100644
--- a/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-pasemi-core.c
+++ b/drivers/i2c/busses/i2c-pasemi-core.c
@@ -196,24 +196,29 @@ static int pasemi_smb_waitready(struct pasemi_smbus *smbus)
 		return -ETIME;
 	}
 
-	/* Still stuck in a transaction? */
-	if (status & SMSTA_XIP) {
-		dev_err(smbus->dev, "Bus stuck, status 0x%08x\n", status);
-		return -EIO;
-	}
-
 	/* Arbitration loss? */
 	if (status & SMSTA_MTA) {
 		dev_err(smbus->dev, "Arbitration loss, status 0x%08x\n", status);
 		return -EBUSY;
 	}
 
-	/* Got NACK? */
+	/*
+	 * Got NACK? The interrupt comes with the NACK, before the controller
+	 * has put the STOP on the bus, so XIP is usually still set here: this
+	 * is checked before XIP, and the transfer is over once the STOP is out
+	 * (the next transfer waits for that).
+	 */
 	if (status & SMSTA_MTN) {
 		dev_err(smbus->dev, "NACK, status 0x%08x\n", status);
 		return -ENXIO;
 	}
 
+	/* Still stuck in a transaction? */
+	if (status & SMSTA_XIP) {
+		dev_err(smbus->dev, "Bus stuck, status 0x%08x\n", status);
+		return -EIO;
+	}
+
 	/* Clear XEN */
 	reg_write(smbus, REG_SMSTA, SMSTA_XEN);
 
